        "id": 1425418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-03T17:02:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That approach would definitely save initial emulator coding time.\n\nI\u0027m concerned that the implicit nature of the array approach could lead to difficult to detect/diagnose emulator/driver bugs, especially if the driver changes. One advantage of the albeit verbose and tedious approach is that any fall through for unhandled registers will hit a default return of -EINVAL; I consider this an advantage because it\u0027s more explicit about what registers we\u0027ve thought about.\n\nFor example: I wouldn\u0027t have considered how SN5S330_INT_STATUS_REG1 cannot be written to (this is only implied by not writing in driver) while SN5S330_INT_STATUS_REG4 could be written to as I likely would not have looked at each individual use-site. If I had forgotten to do that due to the emulator just working, I may have allowed a possible bug in a driver change that eventually does write to SN5S330_INT_STATUS_REG1.\n\nDo you think this is being too proactive about potential driver changes that will likely never happen?",
      "parentUuid": "b0d1330c_ad9c9f4c",
      "revId": "ae0ee78260c79f5d1c538fbd39b864dd79e3e679",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
